WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.110
JENNY BROWN: Hi, I'm Jenny.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.568
And today, I'd like
to show you how

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.590
to use the Apigee
adapter for Envoy

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.039
together with a service mesh to
integrate Envoy-based services

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.540
into your Apigee environment.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.956
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.440
In this demo, I'll show
you how to use Envoy

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.270
to protect a microservice.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.040
I'll add an Envoy
facade on it, which

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.100
is configured to talk to the
Apigee adapter for Envoy.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.260
Let's dive in.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.680
I have a Kubernetes cluster that
has a few applications running.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.360
The one we'll focus
on for this demo

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.450
is the microservice
called Orders.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.580
By default, there
is no restriction

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.160
on who can access
this microservice.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.700
Let me demonstrate that by
accessing it as a consumer.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.550
To simulate a consumer,
I have a cURL application

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.860
running in Client.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.050
Once I am logged into
the client application,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.530
I will access the
Orders microservice.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.840
Based on this result,
I was able to access it

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.260
without passing through
any security credentials.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.990
Now, let me walk you through
how you can enforce a security

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.570
policy so that a consumer
must use credentials

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.280
within a service mesh.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.710
To do that, I'll
enable an Envoy filter.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.160
I've created a file that
describes all the security

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.030
policies I want to enforce
on the Orders microservice.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.100
For instance, I'm
enforcing a policy

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.190
that requests must go
through the Apigee adapter.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.350
I'm now applying this
policy via the filter.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.480
Now, I'm going to access the
Orders microservice again

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.335
like I did before.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.990
This time I got an error of
403 forbidden because I didn't

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.700
have a security credential.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.348
Let's fix that.

00:01:52.348 --> 00:01:54.390
We'll do this through the
API products in Apigee.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.140
I've already created
an Orders API product,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:11.020
which is bound to the endpoint
orders running on port 8080.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.940
I've also created
an application that

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.250
allows me to access the
credentials for the Orders

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.580
microservice.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.960
Let's access the Orders
microservice again,

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.580
this time with the API key.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.910
I'm using an API key.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.610
But you could also use
an access token instead.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.510
Success!

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.790
I was able to access
the microservice.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.680
Through all these
interactions, analytics

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.270
are being collected
and transmitted

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.160
to the Apigee control panel.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.050
Looking at the API
metrics here, you'll

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.790
notice that the
Orders microservice is

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.850
getting requests, and I
can view the data for that.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.520
This is how you can use the
Apigee adapter for Envoy

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.950
to enforce policies for
any service that's using

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.700
Envoy within a service mesh.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.700
If you found this demo
helpful, check out

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.460
our introductory Apigee
adapter for Envoy video linked

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.330
in the description below.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.020
And for more information like
code labs and blog posts,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.500
check out these links.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.310
Thanks for watching.

